"<span>Blind: hide the ionization graph, the line energies, the cutoff and the half value layers (read the spectrum with the cursor, the chamber with the sheets)</span>",
"Shells: each electron in an atom has four quantum numbers n, l, m_l and m_s, and no two can share all four (the Pauli exclusion principle, OpenStax Vol. {#} §{#}). So a subshell holds {#}(2l + {#}) electrons ({#}, {#}, {#}, {#} for s, p, d, f) and a shell 2n². Each box is one orbital (one m_l), holding at most one spin up and one spin down electron; within a subshell the boxes fill once with spin up before any pairs (Hund's rule). \"By the n + l rule\" fills subshells in order of n + l, lower n first on a tie (1s 2s 2p 3s 3p 4s 3d 4p 5s 4d 5p 6s 4f 5d 6p 7s 5f 6d 7p). \"Measured ground state\" is the configuration NIST lists; about twenty elements (chromium, copper, palladium, gadolinium, …) differ from the rule, and those subshells are outlined. The rows are drawn by n, not by energy. Z_eff = n√(IE/{#} eV) treats the outer electron as if it were in a hydrogen atom with nuclear charge Z_eff, a rough estimate.",
"X-ray tube: electrons from a hot filament are accelerated through V and stop in the target. Slowing down in the target, they emit a continuous spectrum (bremsstrahlung) with energy per unit photon energy ∝ Z(E₀ − E) (Kramers' law for a thick target), ending at E₀ = eV, the shortest wavelength λ_min = hc/eV (the Duane–Hunt limit). Per unit wavelength it becomes ∝ (λ/λ_min − {#})/λ³. If eV exceeds the K edge, electrons can knock out a K electron, and an L or M electron falls into the hole: Kα₁ (L₃ to K), Kα₂ (L₂ to K) and Kβ₁ (M₃ to K). The line energies and K edges are measured values; the level diagram's L₃, L₂ and M₃ energies are the K edge minus each line energy. Line heights are drawn, not computed: Kα₂ half of Kα₁, Kβ₁ a fifth, all growing as (U − {#})^{#} with U = eV/E_K, which only roughly follows real tubes; Kβ₃ (beside Kβ₁), L lines and absorption in the target and the window, which removes the long wavelength end of a real spectrum, are left out. The spectrometer gives each line a width of {#}% of its energy, enough to separate Kα₁ and Kα₂ for most targets. For elements that are not solid metals, the target is a compound of the element. The 3D picture is a glass Coolidge tube of typical proportions, not one particular tube; the electrons and X-rays move slowed down for the eye, and each X-ray drawn has an energy picked from the spectrum in the graph, counting photons (intensity divided by energy) and each whole line peak, above a {#} keV cutoff that stands in for the window (red for a Kα photon, blue for Kβ, gray for bremsstrahlung). Filtration and half value layer (for the Medical Physics module): with a filter or sheets in the beam of the tungsten target, from {#} to {#} kV, the graph and the chamber use a full tube model instead of Kramers' law: the spectrum of SpekPy {#}.4 (Poludniowski et al. {#}, Med. Phys. {#}, {#}; validated against measured spectra and half value layers by Bujila, Omar and Poludniowski {#}, Phys. Med. {#}, {#}), a tungsten anode at {#}° behind a {#} mm beryllium window, in {#} keV bins with its K and L lines. That spectrum includes the absorption of the X-rays in the target itself, which Kramers' law leaves out and which removes much of the low energy end. It is the same spectrum, and the same coefficients, that Photon Interactions in Matter uses, so the two demos give the same half value layers. The filters remove photons as e^(−μx), with μ/ρ of aluminum and copper the sums of the NIST XCOM cross sections, interpolated log–log (densities {#} and {#} g/cm³). The ion chamber reads air kerma, K ∝ Σ Φ(E) T(E) E (μ_en/ρ)_air over the bins, each taken at its center energy, with μ_en/ρ for dry air from NIST Standard Reference Database {#}; the reading is relative to no sheets. The half value layer is the aluminum that halves this reading, found by bisection, and the second one is the extra aluminum that halves it again; the mean photon energy is weighted by photon number. These agree with SpekPy's own to {#} %. The tube has no glass envelope or oil in the model, so with {#} mm Al at {#} kV it gives a first HVL of {#} mm Al, below the {#} mm Al minimum that {#} CFR {#}(m) sets for systems made since June {#}; a real tube's inherent filtration adds to it. Other targets and lower voltages draw the filtered Kramers spectrum but give no chamber reading or half value layers, since the shared SpekPy spectra are for tungsten from {#} kV only. In 3D each X-ray of the filtered tungsten beam has an energy picked from the SpekPy spectrum (red for Kα, blue for Kβ, gray for the continuum and the L lines).",
"Build an atom: the number of protons fixes the element; protons plus neutrons give the mass number A; protons less electrons give the charge in units of e (OpenStax Vol. {#} §{#}). Whether that isotope exists, is stable, its half life and natural abundance, and the mass of the neutral atom (A u plus the mass excess) come from NUBASE2020 and AME2020 (F. G. Kondev et al. and M. Wang et al., Chinese Physics C {#}, {#} and {#} ({#})). The electrons fill the measured ground state of the neutral atom; a positive ion loses its outermost electrons first, highest n and then highest l, so Fe³⁺ is [Ar] 3d⁵, and a negative ion takes the places of the neutral atom with as many electrons. That rule fits the common ions; a few ions of the heavier transition metals differ. The rings are shells, not orbits, and the nucleus is drawn far too large. Arrow keys on the picture add and remove protons (up, down) and neutrons (right, left); + and − add and remove electrons.",
"Data: ground configurations and first ionization energies from the NIST Atomic Spectra Database (version {#}); ionization energies are given for Z = {#} to {#}, and configurations above {#} are predicted. X-ray line energies (Kα₁, Kα₂, Kβ₁) and K edges from the NIST X-Ray Transition Energies Database (Deslattes et al., Rev. Mod. Phys. {#}, {#}, {#}), direct measurements; targets whose values NIST interpolates or flags (P, Tc, Pm, Lu, Hf and Z above {#}) are not offered, nor sodium and magnesium, whose atoms have no 3p electron for Kβ₁. hc = {#} eV·nm (CODATA {#}). Element families and the table layout are those of the Spectra Explorer.",
